
EDIF to SUE file translator

USAGE

"edif2sue" is a tool for translating an EDIF design file
into a set of SUE cell files.  

A usage message is available by invoking without parameters.
The most basic usage is:

	edif2sue ediffile.edf

which (if ediffile.edf is a valid edif file) will create a
set of files in the local subdirectory "sue", which should
exist already.

The translation process consists of two parts, which 
are described below.
	1. reading-in and parsing the EDIF file
	2. extraction and writing-out SUE files.

A tool to write EDIF files from sue is available as
a plug-in option to SUE; see "sue2edif" documentation elsewhere.


OPTIONS

Options are specified in a "e2s_rc" file.  The default file is
$MMI_TOOLS/edif2sue/e2s_rc.

An explicit path can be specified using the "-config_file <path> 
command line option.  Any number of paths can be specified;
files will be read in the order specified.

(calling edif2sue with no arguments will cause a usage messge
to be printf'd)

Within the e2src file, options are specified one per line.
The fields of the line are read using the scanf; see the man page.
The first field give the option keyword, as specified below;
following fields are paramaters of the option, if required.


	cellname_prefix:  A complete design can be expected to
contain low-level components such as transistors or simple gates whose
name may collide with similar but probably not identical components in
the local library.  That will create an impossible situation if the
intent is to edit the files.  Therefore the user should specify a
prefix that will be applied to all the cell names extracted from the
edif file (and consequently on the generated sue files).  So for
example, if 
		cellname_prefix fred_
a cell named "inverter" would be renamed to fred_inverter, and its
definition would appear in a file called fred_inverter.sue.

	outdir: Gives a relative or absolute pathname of a directory
where the generated sue files will be placed. The default is a local
subdirectory named "sue". 

	suppress_progress_messages: ordinarily the tool will 
report what cell it is working on, which is reassuring and helpful
for debugging, but may obscure important warning messages.
This option will supress progress reports; only warning messages
will be displayed.

	continue_on_translate_errors: ordinarly the tool will 
abort with an error message when it encounters a situation it doesn't
understand.  Specifying this option will cause the tool to continue;
obviously results will be unpredictable; a segmentation exception or
invalid output may occur.

	literal_netnames: Net names syntax is somewhat variable
among CAD tools.  Ordinarily edif2sue will convert the names into 
a format acceptable to sue.  Note that this may require generating
"buscombine" psuedo-devices and additional generated names.
The generated files may be more readable if the original netnames are 
presented exactly as they are in EDIF, which this option will
do; but note that in this case the generated files may not be
netlistable.

	ignore_property <property_name>: It may be found that some
properties that are not useful for Sue cause excessive clutter;
ie, the Cadence port_order property.  This option causes all instances
of the named propert to be ignored.  The option should be specified
as often as required to ignore multiple properties, eg:
		ignore_property pin
		ignore_property schType
		ignore_property instancesLastChanged
		ignore_property lastSchematicExtraction

	use_global <cell_name> <global_name> {<orientation>}:
This option says that an instance of <cell_name> should be treated
as an instance of the Sue global <global_name>.  The optional orientation
paramater specifies how the global symbol should be displayed. eg,
		use_global VDD! vdd RXY
says that a reference to "VDD!" turns into a Sue global called "vdd",
and the symbol is displayed "upside down" (above its connection point).

	parse_only: don't translate the file, just parse it
and reconstitute the file to stdout. This has been occasionally
useful for debugging.  It also leads to uniform prettyprinting.

	skip_bad_ports: we have discovered some Cadence files that
contain duplicate portimplementations for some net bundle signals.
For example, a cell might have a port called DIN<7:0> (actually a 
rename of this value), and portimplementations for DIN<3:0>, 
DIN<7>, and whatever, in addition to a portimpelemntation for 
DIN<7:0>.  This is quite against the EDIF standard, but what can 
one do?  This hackaround just hopes that if the tool can't find 
a port name that exactly matches the portimplementation reference,
it is an instance of this case and can be ignored.  Obviously the
option should only be invoked if the EDIF file is observed to 
exhibit this anomoly.  If a port is skipped, a warning message is
printf'd.

	ignore_property <property_name>: properties with this name
will be silently skipped over wherever encountered.  This is for
clutter reduction, and also makes it easier to produce netlists
usable by SPICE.

	use_global <cell_name> <global_name> <orientation>:
Rather than create an instance of a cell with this name (such as
"VSS!"), link this net with the MMI global_name (such as "gnd").
If orientation is specified, apply that transformation to the 
global symbol, eg so that vdd symbols will hang down from the 
top of the schematic as usually expected.

	add_verilog_property_by_library <library-name>: add a verilog
property to cells in the named library, identifying them as base
components to the verilog simulator.

	round_to_grid_lines: snap connection points to the nearest
grid lines.  WARNING, In some cases this may pull connections apart.

	add_title_bar: causes a title bar to be added to each cell

	suppress_instance_name_prefix: causes instance names
that begin with the specified string to be suppressed; for 
edif files generated from sue, that prefix is "THING_".

	ingore_cell_definition: if a definition for the named cell 
is encountered, don't create a SUE file for it.  If an instance
of the cell is encountered in the definition for some other cell,
just write it out and hope SUE will know what to do.  

	scale_coordinates: multiply all coordinates encountered by
the given factor.  This might help get a translated schematic on-grid.
(Note: in the ordinary course of things, edif2sue will issue a 
warning if it encounters a coordinate that is off-grid.)

	uniquify_duplicate_names: duplicate names within the same
scope are supposed to be illegal in EDIF, but at least one known 
edif writer doesn't respect this for power nets.  This option will
cause a uniquifying string ("__77nn") to be appended to detected
duplicate names.

Cadence-specific options:

	no_skip_bad_ports: In the case of "net splitter" type constructs,
the subscripts on a portRef may not match those on the intended 
corresponding portDef; strictly speaking this is a violation of the
EDIF standard, but since we have enough information to do the right
thing, we go ahead an connect the nets as probably intended.  If this
option is specified, warning messages will be printf'd when a 
mismatch is encountered, for human verification if desired.

	cadence_property_conversion: cadence properties sometimes
use a macro-substitution language; that is, they are not intended
to be treated as the simple strings which they appear to be in the
EDIF syntax.  If this option is specified, we make a stab at 
interpreting the property.

	show_rippers: A Cadence "ripper"
is a pseudo-device with one port that extracts a subset from a 
bus or net bundle.  Since it has only one port, Sue believes that
the two sides of the ripper are shorted together, with bad
results.  Therefore edif2sue normally suppresses the rippers and
the wire segments connected to them.  Sue matches the nets by 
name, so the schematic produced is equivalent.  If it is desired
to view the schematics exactly as they are presented in the 
EDIF, this option should be invoked; note that in this case the
generated files may not be netlistable.




PARSING

The file is read in line-by-line.  EDIF expressions are 
converted to a direct internal representation in a 
recursive top-down fashion; there is very little compaction.

Each expression is read in according to the definitions in
the standard; errors such as missing required fields or 
illegal fields will be detected and an error message 
identifying the line number of the error is produced.

Name references are resolved as they are encountered in a 
completely correct manner.  Naming errors will generate error 
messages.

There is no error recovery; a parsing error is immediately
fatal. 

The process if fairly memory-intensive; experience shows that
the internal representation is about as large as the input
file.  It is helpful to have that much physical memory 
available.  A large file can take 15-30 minutes to read in.


EXTRACTION

Once parsing has completed successfully, edif2sue will 
walk over the tree of expressions looking for cell definitions,
for all of which it will generate sue files.

Every effort has been made to make this process robust; that
is, to check that the EDIF structure is not just syntacticly
correct, but is what we expect. In cases where the tool is
surprised, as of this writing it will exit with with a message
	    fatal error called
giving an error number and a possibly helpful message;
at this point one should send for help.  

(One can also specify the "-continue_on_translate_errors" option
as a stopgap, but this is obviously dangerous.)(Parsing errors
cannot be continued.)

Some questionable but non-fatal situations will be noted in 
the standard output, which should be examined carefully when
a new design is translated.  The option "-suppress_progress_messages"
will make warning messages stand out more clearly.


EDIF INFORMATION

The EDIF supported is version 2 0 0 (EIA Recommended Standard
EIA-548-1988, dated March 14, 1988.  Some information is 
available from the EDIF Technical Center Web site; see
http://edif-tc.cs.man.ac.uk/index.html.  Only "level 0"
("the basic level - only literal constants are permitted")
is supported; levels 1 and 2 ("addition of parameters and
expressions" and "addition of control-flow constructs")
is not supported.

As of this writing, edif2sue will not give complete coverage
for all legal EDIF constructs; however, meaningful error 
messages should be produced in cases where the tool becomes
confused.  

There is also some question about how various CAD tools map
their internal databases into EDIF; there is plenty of room
for idiosyncracies or outright incorrectness.  The following
have been seen:

    CADENCE:
	- net "joined" structure is not right for nets with
	complex names (see Cadence documentation on 
	"iterated names" to understand how they have created
	a difficult problem for themselves)
	(for edif2sue, we use the "figure" drawing information
	to generate the schematics; we are unable to cross-check
	the figures against the joins in all cases.)

	- "ripper" cells have only one port (EDIF spec 
	specifies that cell-type RIPPER objects have two ports);
	Cadence has geometrical rules to determine which nets
	connected to a ripper cell are connected to each other.
	(edif2sue suppresses the cells and their attached 
	wire segments)

	- portimplementation references don't always match port
	definitions for net bundles; see above concerning the
	"skip_bad_ports" option 

	- indentation is deplorable
	("et pretty" command will fix it up if desired)

    SYNOPSIS:
	Limited experience, but no discrepancies have been noted)

    TANNER:
	duplicat net names for power nets (as, "VddB")	


    No experience with other CAD tools.




ET TOOL USAGE

The "et" tool is intended to provide some help in inspecting
and manipulating EDIF files, which are often too large for 
ordinary editors to deal with usefully.  Invoking without 
options will provide a usage message.  

The following commands are available:

	-get_cell <cellname> <infile> <outfile>: will search
an EDIF infile for a definition of cell cellname, and write the
definition out to outfile.  It reports the original line number
of the start of the outfile. Extremely helpful when parsing
fails a gazillion lines into a large EDIF file.

	-pretty <infile> <outfile>: will prettyprint an
EDIF file by adjusting the indentation, preserving the 
line numbers.

	-break_libs <infile>: write each library encountered
in the infile out to a separate file called lib_n.  Unfortunately
you can't do much with a library except paste it back into 
an EDIF file, but one can at least construct a shortened 
version 

	 
